
▸ Wave 3 — composite keyset cursors (A: pure · B: live PostgreSQL)

  — A. cursor algebra
  ✅ A1 the cursor encodes every ORDER BY column, in order
  ✅ A2 the keyset predicate is the row-value comparison for (date DESC, id ASC)
  ✅ A3 no cursor value is interpolated into the SQL — only $n placeholders
  ✅ A3b the builder binds the trailing id as a number, not a string
  ✅ A4 a leading ASC column flips to `>`, proving direction comes from the spec
  ✅ A5 a bare numeric cursor still works (backwards compatible)
  ✅ A6 a malformed cursor yields no predicate rather than broken SQL
  ✅ A7 a row missing a cursor column falls back to the id-only cursor
  ✅ A8 buildAttendanceList carries a composite keyset spec
  ✅ A9 the composite spec matches the ORDER BY it must mirror
  ✅ A10 a cursor produces the composite predicate in the page SQL
  ✅ A11 the COUNT query never carries a cursor (total must stay stable)
  ✅ A12 students keeps its single-column id cursor (direction-aware, W3-1: ORDER BY id DESC)
  ✅ A12 grades keeps its single-column id cursor (direction-aware, W3-1: ORDER BY id DESC)
  ✅ A12 classes keeps its single-column id cursor (direction-aware, W3-1: ORDER BY id DESC)
  ✅ A12 users keeps its single-column id cursor (direction-aware, W3-1: ORDER BY id DESC)
  ✅ A13 executePagedList emits a composite next_cursor
  ✅ A14 has_more still comes from LIMIT+1

